  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nThrough mid next week (Wednesday)...\n\nToday (Thursday) and Friday: RAP/water vapor analysis indicates \nstagnant upper pattern: remnant upper low losing influence/washing\nout over the Southeast states this morning and 596-597DM mean \nridge aloft over the Desert Southwest. Heights are building with \nlight and variable low level flow gradually shifting southerly \nthrough Friday. This locks in PWs generally around one inch (1.2 \ninches) and low humidity aloft (i.e. 700-300mb layer humidity in \nmid teens to low 20 percent range) in east to northeast MS while \ndeeper moisture (1.6 to 1.8 inches) over central to southern MS \nand portions of northeast LA. Deep moisture will spread areawide \ninto Friday. Increased warm advection (WAA) will moderate \ntemperatures: highs becoming seasonably warm into Wednesday (95F \nto 97F) and seasonable lows (70F to 75F this morning and again \ntonight). Heat indices will continue to increase today in central \nto southwestern areas (heat indices in the 102F to 107F range this\nafternoon) and areas along and west of Interstate 55 Friday. \nInherited HWO graphics for Limited and Elevated heat in southwest \ntoday seems reasonable. With moist advection picking up and lapse \nrates in northeast LA to south central to southwest MS and \nnortheast LA, some diurnally induced showers and isolated storms \nmay develop. \n\nThis weekend through mid next week (Saturday through next \nWednesday): Synoptic pattern will shift to deepening mean ridge \nover the Four Corners while the southeast states caught in \nbetween ridge to the west and subtropical ridge off the Atlantic \nseaboard. Surface boundary layer moisture will increase/higher \nsurface dewpoints this weekend (72F to 77F). Combined with \nseasonable warmth (highs 93F to 96F), heat stress will only spread\nand increase in area, continuing into early next week. HWO \ngraphic looks good for the weekend and continued the threat into \nearly next week (Tuesday). Further refinements and heat headlines \nwill likely be needed.\n\nLow level moist advection and increased deep moisture aloft will \nincrease this the weekend (PWs in the 1.7 to 2.1 inch range late \nweekend into next week). Rain chances will mainly be summertime \nvariety (diurnally induced) Thursday south of Interstate 20 and \nalong and southeast of Natchez Trace into the weekend and \nspreading northwest into early next week. Coverage will be \nisolated to at times scattered coverage (20 to 40 percent range) \nwith highest coverage in the Pine Belt, with potential sea breeze \ninfluence. This pattern does not favor noteworthy organized storm \nthreat, but scattered stronger gusty storms with locally heavy \nrain cannot be ruled out, especially late weekend through mid next\nweek. /DC/",
